WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:10.590
I can still remember the faces when I suggested a method of dealing with what most teachers of English considered one of their pet horrors, extended reading.
我至今还记得自己提出一种方法来应对拓展阅读时大家脸上的表情——大多数英语教师都把拓展阅读视为自己最头疼的事情之一。

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:21.200
The room was full of tired teachers, and many were quite cynical about the offer to work together to create a new and dynamic approach to the place of stories in the classroom.
屋里满是疲惫的教师，许多人对这项提议相当怀疑：大家一起合作，开创一种全新而富有活力的方法，来看待故事在课堂上的地位。

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.670
They had seen promises come and go, and mere words weren’t going to convince them, which was a shame as it was mere words that we were principally dealing with.
他们见惯了各种承诺许下又落空，光凭言辞是说服不了他们的。这就可惜了，因为我们主要打交道的恰恰只是言辞。

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:45.630
Most teachers were unimpressed by the extended reading challenge from the Ministry, and their lack of enthusiasm for the rather dry list of suggested tales was passed on to their students and everyone was pleased when that part of the syllabus was over.
大多数教师对教育部提出的拓展阅读挑战不以为然；那份推荐故事的书目相当枯燥，他们对此缺乏热情，这种情绪也传给了学生；等教学大纲里这一部分结束了，人人都很高兴。

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.590
It was simply a box-ticking exercise.
那只不过是一件打钩了事、走走过场的差事。

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.100
We needed to do something more.
我们需要再多做点什么。

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.840
We needed a very different approach.
我们需要一种截然不同的方法。

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.080
That was ten years ago.
那是十年前的事了。

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.720
Now we have a different approach, and it works.
如今我们有了一种不同的方法，而且很管用。

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:01.770
Here’s how it happened.
下面就讲讲事情的经过。

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.940
(Or, as with most good stories, here are the main parts.
（或者说，就像大多数好故事一样，下面是其中的主要部分。

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:13.070
You have to fill in some of it yourself, employing that underused classroom device, the imagination.)
有些内容你得自己补全，运用想象力——这个在课堂上未得到充分利用的手段。）

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:22.220
We—that is, the British Council in Portugal and the Portuguese teachers’ association APPI—started with three main precepts:
我们——也就是英国文化协会葡萄牙分部和葡萄牙教师协会APPI——从三条主要原则出发：

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:28.030
First, it is important to realise that all of us are storytellers, tellers of tales.
第一，重要的是要认识到，我们所有人都是讲故事的人，都是故事的讲述者。

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:39.150
We all have our own narratives—the real stories such as what happened to us this morning or last night, and the ones we have been told by others and we haven’t experienced personally.
我们每个人都有自己的叙事：既有真实的故事，比如今天早上或昨晚我们身上发生了什么；也有别人讲给我们听、我们并未亲身经历过的故事。

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.490
We could say that our entire lives are constructed as narratives.
可以说，我们的整个人生都是以叙事的形式构建起来的。

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:49.060
As a result, we all understand and instinctively feel narrative structure.
因此，我们都理解叙事结构，也能本能地感知它。

00:01:49.860 --> 00:02:04.150
Binary opposites—for example, the tension created between good and bad together with the resolution of that tension through the intervention of time, resourcefulness and virtue—is a concept understood by even the youngest children.
二元对立——例如善与恶之间产生的张力，以及这种张力借助时间、机智和美德的介入而得到化解——是一个连最小的孩子都理解的概念。

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:14.130
Professor Kieran Egan, in his seminal book ‘Teaching as Story Telling’, warns us not to ignore this innate skill, for it is a remarkable tool for learning.
基兰·伊根教授在其影响深远的著作《教学即讲故事》中告诫我们，不要忽视这种与生俱来的技能，因为它是一种非凡的学习工具。

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:29.460
We need to understand that writing and reading are two sides of the same coin: an author has not completed the task if the book is not read: the creative circle is not complete without the reader, who will supply their own creative input to the process.
我们需要明白，写作和阅读是一枚硬币的两面：书如果没人读，作者就没有完成任务；没有读者，创作的循环就不完整，读者会为这一过程作出自己的创造性贡献。

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.660
Samuel Johnson said: ‘A writer only begins a book.
塞缪尔·约翰逊说过：“作者只是开了一本书的头，

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:35.800
A reader finishes it.’
读者则把它完成。”

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:46.960
In teaching terms, we often forget that reading itself can be a creative process, just as writing is, and we too often relegate it to a means of data collection.
从教学的角度来说，我们常常忘记，阅读本身也可以是一个创造性的过程，就像写作一样；而且我们动不动就把阅读降格为收集资料的手段。

00:02:47.760 --> 00:03:04.290
We frequently forget to make that distinction when presenting narratives or poetry, and often ask comprehension questions which relate to factual information—who said what and when, rather than speculating on ‘why’, for example, or examining the context of the action.
我们在讲授叙事作品或诗歌时，经常忘了做这种区分，而且常常提一些涉及事实信息的理解题——谁在什么时候说了什么，而不是去推测诸如“为什么”之类的问题，或者审视情节发生的背景。

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:18.320
The third part of the reasoning that we adopted relates to the need to engage the students as readers in their own right, not simply as language learners; learning the language is part of the process, not the reason for reading.
我们所采纳的思路，第三部分涉及的是：需要让学生作为真正意义上的读者投入阅读，而不仅仅是作为语言学习者；学习语言是这一过程的一部分，而不是阅读的理由。

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:26.690
What they read must become theirs and have its own special and secret life in their heads, a place where teachers can only go if invited.
学生读的东西必须变成他们自己的，在他们的脑海中拥有自己独特而隐秘的生命；那是教师只有受到邀请才能进入的地方。

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:36.960
We quickly found that one of the most important ways of making all the foregoing happen was to engage the creative talents of the class before they read a word of the text.
我们很快发现，要让上述一切得以实现，最重要的途径之一，就是在全班学生连文本的一个字都还没读的时候，就调动起他们的创造才能。

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:51.300
The pre-reading activities become the most important part of the teaching process; the actual reading part can almost be seen as the icing on the cake, and the principal aim of pre-reading activities is to get students to want to read the text.
读前活动成为教学过程中最重要的部分；实际阅读的部分几乎可以看作锦上添花，而读前活动的主要目的是让学生想要阅读文本。

00:03:52.100 --> 00:04:04.980
We developed a series of activities which use clues or fragments from the text yet to be read, and which rely on the student’s innate knowledge of narrative, so that they can build their own stories before they read the key text.
我们设计了一系列活动，这些活动利用待读文本中的线索或片段，并依靠学生天生具备的叙事知识，好让他们在阅读核心文本之前构建出自己的故事。

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:15.750
They have enough information to generate ideas but not so much that it becomes simply an exercise in guided writing; releasing a free imagination is the objective.
他们得到的信息足以激发想法，但又不至于多到让活动变成单纯的引导式写作练习；目标是释放自由的想象力。

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:21.580
Moving from pre-reading to reading, we may introduce textual intervention activities.
从读前阶段进入阅读阶段后，我们可能会引入文本干预活动。

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:41.490
‘Textual Intervention’ is a term used by Rob Pope to describe the process of questioning a text not simply as a guide to comprehension but as a way of exploring the context of the story at any one time, and examining points at which the narrative presents choices, points of divergence, or narrative crossroads.
“文本干预”是罗布·波普使用的一个术语，用来描述这样一个过程：对文本提出疑问，不仅仅把提问当作理解的指引，更把它当作一种方式，借以探究故事在任一时刻的背景，审视叙事在哪些节点上呈现出选择、分岔点或叙事的十字路口。

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:50.530
We don’t do this for all texts, however, as the shorter ones do not seem to gain much from this process and it simply breaks up the reading pleasure.
不过，我们并不是对所有文本都这样做，因为较短的文本似乎从这一过程中获益不多，而且这样做只会破坏阅读的乐趣。

00:04:51.330 --> 00:05:04.530
Follow-up activities are needed, at the least, to round off the activity, to bring some sense of closure, but they also offer an opportunity to link the reading experience more directly to the requirements of the syllabus.
需要有后续活动，最起码用来给活动收尾，带来某种完结感；不过，后续活动还提供了一个机会，能把阅读体验更直接地同教学大纲的要求联系起来。

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:21.920
Indeed, the story may have been chosen in the first place because the context supports one of the themes that teachers are required to examine as part of the syllabus—for example, ‘families’, ‘science and technology’, ‘communications’, ‘the environment’ and all the other familiar themes.
事实上，当初选择这个故事，可能就是因为它的背景契合教学大纲中要求教师探讨的某个主题——比如“家庭”“科学技术”“交流”“环境”以及其他所有熟悉的主题。

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.660
There are very few stories that can be explored without some part of the syllabus being supported.
能够拿来探讨、却又不配合教学大纲任何部分的故事少之又少。

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:34.630
For many teachers this is an essential requirement if they are to engage in such extensive reading at all.
对许多教师来说，这是一项必不可少的要求，不然他们根本不会去开展这样的泛读。

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:42.900
The whole process—pre-, while- and post-reading—could be just an hour’s activity, or it could last for more than one lesson.
整个过程——读前、读中和读后——可能只是一个小时的活动，也可能持续不止一节课。

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:57.410
When we are designing the materials for exploring stories, clearly it isn’t possible for us to know how much time any teacher will have available, which is why we construct the activities into a series of independent units which we call kits.
我们在设计探讨故事的材料时，显然无法知道任何一位教师会有多少时间可用，正因如此，我们把这些活动编排成一系列独立的单元，并称之为套件。

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:07.160
They are called kits because we expect teachers to build their own lessons out of the materials we provide, which implies that large amounts may be discarded.
之所以称为套件，是因为我们希望教师用我们提供的材料搭建自己的课，这就意味着可能会有大量材料被舍弃。

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:13.450
What we do ask, though, is that the pre-reading activities be included, if nothing else.
不过，我们确实有一个要求：哪怕别的都不用，也要把读前活动包括进去。

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.630
That is essential for the process to engage the student as a creative reader.
要让这一过程吸引学生作为创造性的读者投入阅读，读前活动必不可少。

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:27.270
One of the purposes of encouraging a creative reading approach in the language classroom is to do with the dynamics we perceive in the classroom.
在语言课堂上提倡创造性阅读方法，目的之一与我们在课堂上感知到的互动关系有关。

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:36.140
Strategic theorists tell us of the social trinity, whereby three elements are required to achieve a dynamic in any social situation.
战略理论家向我们介绍了社会三位一体：在任何社会情境中，都需要三个要素才能形成互动。

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:42.710
In the language classroom these might be seen as consisting of the student, the teacher and the language.
在语言课堂上，这三个要素或许可以看作由学生、教师和语言构成。

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:55.260
Certainly from the perspective of the student—and usually from the perspective of the teacher—the relationship is an unequal one, with the language being perceived as placed closer to the teacher than the student.
无疑，从学生的角度看——而且通常从教师的角度看也是如此——这种关系是不平等的：在他们看来，语言离教师比离学生更近。

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:01.500
This will result in less dynamic between language and student than between language and teacher.
这会导致语言与学生之间的互动少于语言与教师之间的互动。

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:23.090
However, if we replace ‘language’ with ‘narrative’ and especially if that is approached as a creative process that draws the student in so that they feel they ‘own’ the relationship with the text, then this will shift the dynamic in the classroom so that the student, who has now become a reader, is much closer to the language—or narrative—than previously.
然而，如果我们把“语言”换成“叙事”，尤其是如果把叙事当作一个创造性的过程来对待，吸引学生投入其中，让他们觉得与文本之间的关系“属于”自己，那么这就会改变课堂上的互动，让此时已成为读者的学生比以前离语言——或者说叙事——近得多。

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:26.770
This creates a much more effective dynamic of learning.
这就形成了一种有效得多的学习互动。

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:32.880
However, some teachers feel threatened by this apparent loss of overall control and mastery.
然而，这看上去意味着教师失去了对全局的控制和主导，一些教师因此感到受到了威胁。

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:43.550
Indeed, the whole business of open-ended creativity and a lack of boxes to tick for the correct answer is quite unsettling territory for some to find themselves in.
事实上，开放式的创造，再加上没有方框可以给正确答案打钩，这一整套做法会让一些人觉得自己置身于一片相当令人不安的领域。
